      title: "Book 4: The Happiness of the Whole City",
      body: `## Adeimantus objects

Book 4 opens with an objection. **Adeimantus** points out that the guardians, as Socrates has described them, will not be very **happy**. They will own the city, yet they will have no land, no fine houses, no gold or silver, nothing to spend on travel or on mistresses, nothing to offer the gods in private sacrifices. They will be like hired mercenaries, sitting in the city with nothing to do but guard it (419a–420a).

Socrates replies with one of the central principles of the *Republic*:

> "We are not founding the city so that any one group in it will be exceptionally happy, but so that the **whole city** will be as happy as possible. … Just as, if we were painting a statue and someone came and blamed us for not putting the most beautiful colors on the most beautiful parts of the animal, since the eyes, which are the most beautiful part, had not been painted purple but black, we would think it a fair answer to say: 'You must not expect us to paint the eyes so beautifully that they no longer look like eyes, … but consider whether, by giving each part what is appropriate, we make the whole beautiful.'" (*Republic* 4.420b–d, plain rendering)

If the guardians were given luxuries, they would cease to be guardians, as farmers dressed in fine robes and crowned with gold would cease to be farmers. A city in which the guardians were not true guardians would be destroyed. So each class must be given the happiness appropriate to its nature and its role in the whole (420e–421c).

This principle, that the good of the **whole** comes before the good of any part, is one of the most debated aspects of the *Republic*. Critics, from **Aristotle** onward, have objected that a city cannot be happy unless its citizens are happy: "if the guardians are not happy, who else is?" (*Politics* 2.5, 1264b15–25). Defenders of Plato reply that Socrates does not deny the guardians happiness, only luxurious happiness, and that in Book 9 he will argue that the life of the just philosopher is in fact the happiest of all.

## Wealth and poverty

Socrates then explains that the guardians must prevent two things from entering the city: **wealth** and **poverty**. Wealth produces luxury, idleness, and innovation; poverty produces meanness and bad workmanship, and both produce discontent (421c–422a). A potter who becomes rich will no longer care about his craft; one who is too poor to buy tools will do poor work.

But how will a city without wealth defend itself against rich enemies? Socrates answers that trained fighters easily defeat fat, rich men; and that the city can always make allies by offering them the spoils of war, since it has no use for gold or silver itself. Moreover, every other city is really **two cities**, at war with each other, the city of the rich and the city of the poor, and one can turn them against each other (422a–423a).

## Unity

The city's greatest concern must be its **unity**. It should grow only as large as it can while remaining **one** (423b–c). And the guardians must ensure that each citizen is placed in the class for which his nature fits him, so that "each person, practicing his one job, may become not many but one, and so the whole city may grow to be one and not many" (423d).

## The primacy of education

All of this, Socrates says, depends on one great thing: **education** and **upbringing** (423e). If the guardians are well educated, they will understand these matters for themselves, and many others that Socrates has passed over, such as marriage and the procreation of children, which must be arranged, as the proverb says, "friends have all things in common" (424a), a hint of Book 5.

The guardians must watch over education above all, and permit **no innovation** in music and gymnastics, "for when the modes of music change, the fundamental laws of the city change with them," as the musical theorist **Damon** said (424c). Lawlessness creeps in through music as a form of play, and spreads to manners, then to contracts, then to laws and constitutions, "until it overturns everything, public and private" (424d–e).

Many detailed laws, about markets, contracts, insults, lawsuits, and taxes, need not be legislated at all, if the citizens are well educated; and if they are not, no amount of legislation will help. Those who keep passing laws to fix small problems are like sick people who, through lack of self-control, keep trying new medicines instead of giving up their bad way of life; they "do not realize that they are really cutting off a **Hydra**'s head" (426e). The only laws that the founders must lay down concern **religion**: temples, sacrifices, and the worship of gods and heroes, which they will leave to **Apollo** at Delphi, "the ancestral interpreter" (427b–c).

## The city is founded

"Well, then, son of Ariston," Socrates says to Adeimantus, "your city is now founded" (427c). It remains to find **justice** in it. Socrates proposes that, if the city has been rightly founded, it is **perfectly good**, and so it must be **wise**, **courageous**, **moderate**, and **just**: the four virtues that later tradition would call the **cardinal virtues** (from Latin *cardo*, "hinge," the virtues on which the others turn). If we can find three of them, the one that remains will be justice (427e–428a).

## Ideal and reality

It is worth noticing how much of Socrates's city is drawn from **Sparta**, the great rival of democratic Athens, whose discipline many Athenian aristocrats, including some of Plato's relatives, admired. The professional warrior class, the common messes, the ban on gold and silver, the physical training of women, and the suspicion of new music all have Spartan parallels. But Socrates's city differs from Sparta in decisive ways: its rulers are not the strongest warriors but the wisest, educated in mathematics and philosophy; and its unity rests not only on discipline but on a shared understanding of the good. In Book 8, Socrates will describe a Sparta-like regime, "timocracy," as the first step in the **decline** of the ideal city.

> **Pause and consider.** Socrates says that the city is founded not to make any one class exceptionally happy, but to make the whole city as happy as possible. Can a whole be happy if some of its parts are not? How would you weigh the happiness of the whole against that of individuals?`,
