      title: "Justice in the Soul",
      body: `## The virtues of the soul

With the three parts of the soul established, Socrates can find the four virtues in the **individual**, just as he found them in the city (441c–442d):

- A person is **wise** because of the **rational** part, which has the knowledge of what is good for each part and for the whole soul.
- A person is **courageous** because of the **spirited** part, which holds firm, through pleasures and pains, to what reason has declared is to be feared and what is not.
- A person is **moderate** when all three parts are in **agreement** that reason should rule, and spirit and appetite do not rebel against it.
- And a person is **just** when each part of the soul **does its own work**: reason rules, spirit supports reason, and appetite obeys.

Music and gymnastics, Socrates says, will bring reason and spirit into harmony, "tightening the one and nourishing it with fine speeches and studies, relaxing the other and soothing it, and taming it with harmony and rhythm." Together, they will rule over appetite, "which is the largest part of the soul in each person and by nature most insatiable for money," preventing it from growing so large and strong through bodily pleasures that it tries to enslave the other parts (441e–442b).

## Justice as an inner order

Now Socrates reaches the conclusion toward which the whole argument has been moving:

> "Justice, in truth, … is not concerned with someone's doing his own externally, but with what is **within**, … with what truly concerns himself and his own. He does not allow each part in him to do the work of another, or the classes in the soul to meddle with one another; but he puts his own house truly in order, rules himself, sets himself in order, becomes his own friend, and harmonizes the three parts, exactly like the three notes of a musical scale, the highest, the lowest, and the middle. … And having bound them together and become entirely one out of many, moderate and harmonized, then and only then does he act, whether in acquiring money, or in caring for his body, or in politics or private contracts." (*Republic* 4.443c–e, plain rendering)

This is the heart of the *Republic*'s answer to Thrasymachus and Glaucon. **Justice**, in its truest sense, is not primarily a matter of **external actions**, not stealing, keeping promises, and so on. It is a condition of the **soul**: an inner **harmony**, in which reason rules and the other parts cooperate. Just **actions** are those that produce and preserve this inner harmony; unjust actions are those that destroy it.

## Injustice as civil war

**Injustice**, by contrast, is a kind of **civil war** (*stasis*) in the soul: "a rebellion of one part against the whole soul, to rule in it when it is not fitting" (444b). When appetite or spirit seizes control and overthrows reason, the soul is in disorder: "the confusion and wandering of these parts are injustice, licentiousness, cowardice, ignorance, and, in short, every vice" (444b).

## Justice as health

Socrates then makes the comparison that will answer Glaucon's challenge:

> "Just and unjust actions are no different from healthy and diseased things; as these are in the body, so those are in the soul. … To produce health is to establish the parts of the body in a natural relation of mastering and being mastered by one another; to produce disease is to establish them in a relation of ruling and being ruled contrary to nature. … And to produce justice is to establish the parts of the soul in a natural relation of mastering and being mastered by one another, and to produce injustice is to establish a relation of ruling and being ruled contrary to nature. … So virtue, it seems, is a kind of **health**, beauty, and good condition of the soul, and vice is disease, ugliness, and weakness." (*Republic* 4.444c–e, plain rendering)

If this is right, then Glaucon's question, whether justice is worth having for its own sake, has a clear answer. Justice is to the soul what health is to the body. Who would want to live with a diseased body, even with all the wealth and power in the world? And how much less would anyone want to live with a **diseased soul**, "the very thing by which we live," even if he could do whatever he wished (445a–b)? Glaucon agrees that the question is now "ridiculous." But Socrates says they must go further, and see how many forms of vice there are, which he will do in Books 8–9. Before he can do so, he is interrupted.

## The analogy questioned

Has Socrates really answered Glaucon? Many readers have doubted it. In a famous article of **1963**, "A Fallacy in Plato's *Republic*," the American philosopher **David Sachs** argued that Socrates had changed the subject. Glaucon asked whether **justice** in the **ordinary** sense, not stealing, not lying, not killing, is good for the person who practices it. Socrates answered that **justice** in a **new** sense, the harmony of the soul, is good for its possessor. But he did not show that a person with a harmonious soul will always act justly in the ordinary sense, or that a person who acts justly in the ordinary sense must have a harmonious soul. Could there not be a clever criminal, whose reason firmly rules his appetites, and who calmly calculates that he should commit a crime?

Socrates seems to have an answer, at least in outline. He says that a person with a harmonious soul would never embezzle money, rob temples, steal, betray friends or cities, break oaths, commit adultery, neglect parents, or fail to honor the gods, because these acts come from the domination of appetite or spirit over reason (442e–443b). But this claim depends on Plato's account of what reason **knows**, which he will develop only in Books 6–7, with the theory of the Form of the Good. Scholars still debate whether the argument works.

The British philosopher **Bernard Williams**, in an influential essay of 1973, "The Analogy of City and Soul in Plato's *Republic*," raised another problem: if a city is just when each of its citizens has the appropriate virtue, and the producers are dominated by appetite, then the just city seems to be made up largely of citizens whose souls are **not** just. The relationship between the justice of a city and the justice of its citizens is more complicated than the analogy suggests.

> **Pause and consider.** Socrates argues that justice is the health of the soul, and that no one would want a diseased soul any more than a diseased body. Does this show that justice is good in itself? Could someone have a well-ordered soul and still act unjustly toward others?`,
