      title: "The Second Wave: The Abolition of the Family",
      body: `## No private families

The second wave is larger still. Among the guardians, there will be no private marriages or families:

> "These women shall all be common to all these men, and no woman shall live privately with any man; and the children too shall be common, and no parent shall know his own offspring, nor any child his parent." (*Republic* 5.457c–d, plain rendering)

Socrates must show both that this arrangement is **possible** and that it is **beneficial**. He asks Glaucon to let him put off the question of possibility, and to begin with how it would work and why it would be best (458a–b).

## Breeding the guardians

The rulers will arrange **sexual unions** among the guardians, in order to produce the best possible children, as breeders of hunting dogs and fine birds mate the best with the best (459a–b). This will require the rulers to use "a great many lies and deceptions for the benefit of the ruled" (459c–d). There will be **festivals**, at which brides and grooms are brought together, with sacrifices and hymns. The pairings will appear to be decided by **lot**, but the lots will secretly be rigged by the rulers, so that the best men mate with the best women as often as possible, and the worst with the worst as seldom as possible; those who are dissatisfied will blame chance, not the rulers (459d–460a). Young men who distinguish themselves in war will be given more frequent opportunities to mate, so that they will father more children.

The children of the best will be taken to a **nursery** in a separate part of the city, where nurses will raise them. Mothers will come to nurse them when their breasts are full, but will be prevented from knowing which child is theirs; and the children "of the worse, and any of the others born defective," will be hidden away "in a secret and unknown place, as is fitting" (460c), a phrase that many scholars interpret as a euphemism for infanticide, though others think it refers to placing such children in the lower classes. Guardians will have children only within certain ages: women from twenty to forty, men from about twenty-five to fifty-five (460e). After these ages, they may have sexual relations freely, but any child conceived must not be brought to light (461b–c).

How will the guardians avoid incest? All children born between seven and ten months after a man's marriage festival will be considered his **sons** and **daughters**, and they will call him **father**; their children will be his **grandchildren**; and those born at the same time as a man's own children will be his **brothers** and **sisters**. Marriages between "brothers and sisters" will be permitted if the lot falls that way and Apollo approves (461c–e).

## "Mine" and "not mine"

Why is this arrangement **best**? Socrates's answer is that it produces the greatest possible **unity** in the city. The greatest evil for a city is anything that **divides** it and makes it many instead of one; the greatest good is what **binds** it together. What binds a city together is the sharing of pleasures and pains, when all the citizens rejoice and grieve at the same things. What divides it is when some rejoice and others grieve at the same events (462a–c).

> "Is it not from this, when they do not all say 'mine' and 'not mine' about the same things at the same time? … And the city in which the greatest number say 'mine' and 'not mine' about the same thing, in the same way, is the best governed." (*Republic* 5.462c, plain rendering)

A well-governed city is like a single **body**: when a man's finger is hurt, the whole person feels the pain, and we say "the man has a pain in his finger." So in the best city, when any citizen suffers, the whole city will share his suffering (462c–e). Among the guardians, with no private families or property, every guardian will regard every other as a brother, sister, father, mother, son, or daughter, and they will all say "mine" of the same things. There will be no lawsuits, no factions, no quarrels over property or family (464a–e).

## Aristotle's objection

**Aristotle** criticized this proposal sharply in the second book of his *Politics* (2.1–5). A city, he argued, is by nature a **plurality**, made up of different kinds of people; too much unity would turn it into a household, or a single individual. And Plato's arrangement would not produce more affection, but less: "What is common to the greatest number receives the least care. … Each citizen will have a thousand sons, and these will not be sons of each as an individual; any son will be equally the son of anyone, and so will be neglected by all alike" (*Politics* 2.3, 1261b33–40). "It is better to be one's own cousin than a son in the Platonic way." People care most about what is **their own**, and love is diluted when it is spread too thin, like "a little sweet wine mixed with a great deal of water." Aristotle's critique has been influential in defending the family and private property as the foundations of social life.

## War

Socrates also describes how the guardians will conduct **war** (466e–471c). Children will be taken to watch battles, on horseback, so that they can escape if necessary, as the children of craftsmen watch their parents at work. Those who show cowardice will be demoted to the producing class; those who show courage will be honored, and, Glaucon adds, kissed by everyone on the campaign. Most strikingly, Socrates proposes new rules for war between **Greeks**. Greeks, he says, are by nature kin, and wars among them are really **civil wars** (*stasis*), a sickness of Greece; they should not enslave Greek cities, ravage their land, or burn their houses, but only take the year's harvest, and should fight as those who will one day be reconciled. Toward **barbarians**, however, they may behave as Greeks now behave toward one another (469b–471b). This passage reflects the Panhellenic sentiments of Plato's time, and its distinction between Greeks and barbarians, which modern readers find troubling.

> **Pause and consider.** Socrates argues that a city is best when all its citizens say "mine" of the same things. Aristotle replied that "what is common to the greatest number receives the least care." Who is right? Why do people care most for what is their own?`,
